The function fails because C passes arguments by value. The parameters a and b are local copies stored in swap's activation record. Swapping a and b modifies only those local copies; the caller's variables remain unchanged.

To work correctly, the function must accept pointers to the caller's variables:

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

The caller invokes it passing addresses: swap(&x, &y);

